ISO 226, Table 1
----------------

The per-frequency coefficients in `iso226_utils.py` are reproduced from Table 1
of the ISO 226 equal-loudness-level contour standard,
(c) International Organization for Standardization:

  * `ISO_AF` and `ISO_LU` (29 values each) are from ISO 226:2003.
  * `ISO_TF` (29 values) is from ISO 226:2003, except the 20 Hz threshold,
    which is the 78.1 dB value from ISO 226:2023 (aligned with
    ISO 389-7:2019).

That mixture is an artefact of this project's history rather than a considered
choice, and a correction to the complete ISO 226:2023 coefficient set is
prepared but deliberately unpublished pending the permission described below.

These coefficients are reproduced because ISO 226 Formula (1) cannot be
evaluated without them: with them removed, the software has no function.

**Permission for this reproduction has been requested and is pending.** ISO
customer service confirmed on 1 August 2026 that reproducing Table 1
constitutes reproduction of ISO content, that it requires explicit permission
or a licensing agreement rather than being covered automatically by fair use,
and that such permissions are handled by the national ISO member body -- ANSI
in the United States. A request is with ANSI. **If permission is refused, these
coefficients will be removed and this notice updated to say so.**

Nothing else from the standard is reproduced here beyond short quotations of
the Scope and of the stated range of validity of Formula (1). Annex B is
deliberately excluded.

**This project is not a substitute for the standard.** Anyone doing serious
work with equal-loudness contours should buy it:
https://www.iso.org/standard/83117.html


ISO 266
-------

`ISO_FREQ` is the preferred one-third-octave frequency series of ISO 266,
included so the coefficients above are correctly indexed.
